{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794150538,"nonce":"ZULbi5oJx4d7MP1l705WVmhQKJQ=","message":"2026-11-08, iPhone 12 Pro Max, results of the experiments above and the fix that followed.\n\nPer-feature run (cool-down of 75 s before each launch, steady fps): full shader 34.5 and 34.3; each of border, gradient, inline shadow, shape clip, 3D tilt and mask+filter alone 60.0; all features off 60.0. No single block is slow: the shader slows only when all of them coexist, the signature of register pressure and occupancy, so the cost was paid by fragments that used none of them.\n\nFix, in the working tree: `sdf_core.wgsl` has `override FEAT_*` switches; a second core pipeline turns off gradient, mask, filter, inline shadow, 3D tilt and shape clip (border stays); `GpuPrimitive::is_simple_core` classifies rect, circle and ellipse primitives into `SdfPipelineCategory::CoreSimple`, drawn in contiguous runs so z-order holds. mobile/example: 50 of 52 core primitives are simple. iPhone 60.0 fps at rest and while flinging (471 deceleration ticks at 16.7 ms median, 21 ms worst); the owner confirmed scrolling is smooth. Vulkan (Android emulator) accepts the override constants and renders the same. 245 blinc_gpu tests and 1480 layout, recorder, cn and app tests pass; a unit test pins which primitives count as simple.\n\nStill open here: the shadow pipeline (about 14 primitives, 41 to 46 fps with the real shader against 60 flat, before the core change), a middle variant for primitives with a gradient or inline shadow if a screen shows them mattering, the same switch treatment for the vertex-buffer and data-texture tiers, and a retained scroll layer so unchanged content is not shaded every frame.","files":null}]}